EXAMPLE CALCULATION

Suppose 3 200 ft2 workshop is enclosed by ‘our
walls (]80 ft4), a ceiling (200 ft2) and a floor

(200 ft*), of which 1/2 is covered by furniture or
carpet. The wall 1s 6-in. concrete and the ceiling is
2-in. wood, each with insulation outside. The floor
1s l-in, hardwood laid on a 4-in. concrete slab laid
on earth. The room has 30 ft2 of direct gain win-
dows facing south. Calculate the clear-day winter
tempe, ature swing.

Run the DHC program in order to determine DHC as
Needed to calculate Eq. (5) for aT(swing). The com-
puter printout is shown below, The first step is to
select Menu 2 three times in succe: sion in order to
calculate the dhc of each surface type. The layers
are described working from the outside {nward. The
dhc values shown on each line refer to the dic deter-
mined at the inner surface of each successive layer so
that the final answer on che last line is the cumula-
tive dnc. The last line (airgap) refers to the inside
film coefficient (h = 1.5 Btu/'F ft2 h) and is
therefore the convective dhc. The line above this
gives the radiative dhc. Lag refers to the phase,
which is given ir hours. For example, the radiative
dhc for the floor (surface type 3) is 3.6 Btu/'F fte
with a phase lsy of 1.5 hours (22.5°). Note that the
earth was represente? by adobe pruperties, The choice
of a 24-in. thickness was chosen to represent an es-
sentially infinite thickness for diurnal effects. The
total heat capacity (HC) and total mass are also
listed.

